Spinal Accessory Nerve (CN XI);
- Located in the occipital triangle.
- Runs superficially on the levator scapulae, beneath the investing layer of deep cervical fascia.

• C4 (fourth cervical vertebra);
• Continuous with the trachea below;
• Opens into the laryngopharynx above.

• External laryngeal nerve;
• Supplies the cricothyroid muscle;
• Paralysis prevents tensing of the vocal folds.

• Sole abductor of vocal folds;
• Rotates arytenoid cartilages laterally;
• Widens the rima glottidis for inspiration.

• Vestibular: Fixed, vascular (pink), false folds;
• Vocal: Mobile, avascular (white), true folds;
• Vocal folds contain the vocal ligament and vocalis muscle.

• Artery: Superior laryngeal artery (from superior thyroid);
• Nerve: Internal laryngeal nerve (CN X);
• Pierces the thyrohyoid membrane.

• Cricoid cartilage;
• Shaped like a signet ring;
• Foundation for arytenoid and thyroid articulations.

• Cricothyroid ligament (membrane);
• Located below the vocal folds;
• Safer than tracheostomy for rapid airway access.

• Elevation of the larynx against the tongue;
• Epiglottis moves posteriorly to cap the inlet;
• Contraction of oblique arytenoid and aryepiglottic muscles.

• Above: Internal laryngeal nerve;
• Below: Recurrent (inferior) laryngeal nerve;
• Both are branches of the Vagus nerve (CN X).

• Thyroid cartilage;
• Cricoid cartilage;
• Epiglottic cartilage.
